ontracted to train 60 PhD students in  heritage science we have 55  institutional partners a mixture of  heritage and Industry and there are  three universities collaborating  university college london and i'm the  director of this program the University  of Brighton and the University of Oxford  since sep tember 2014 we have also  leveraged an additional 1 million  dollars from industry and heritage  partners and we have 19 funded  studentships so far so the way that each  each student ship works its valued at  120,000 pounds or about 170,000 dollars  for four years and it's a public-private  partnership two-thirds of the funding is  public partnership and the third is  funded by the industry or heritage  partner and this includes a 3,000 pounds  yearly budget per student for research  expenses and fees and stipends the  tripartite supervision works in the  following way the students currently in  the student ships currently involved 18  companies because two companies are  actually one company is contributing to  two studentships and these can be from  multinational companies to small and  medium-sized companies and and sometimes  they can may be about four or five  employees in the company and 19 heritage  Institute  asians and the tripartite supervision  means that the students would get their  normal academic supervisors which is to  a primary and the secondary supervisor  but in addition they must have a  heritage supervisor and they must have  an industry supervisor and that enables  them to develop their interdisciplinary  and transferable skills which will  enable them to work not only in the  public sector indian universities but  possibly also set up their own  businesses or work with industry so the  projects cover a wide range of topics  under one or more of the following  research strands materials where the  main focus of the students study will be  at University College London environment  where students who major in that area  would go to university of oxford and on  the Digital side where they would go to  the University of Brighton but because  it's a four-year program the first year  is always done as a master's course at  University College London so they sued  ins enjoy quite a unique research  learning experience they have a range of  supervisors from different backgrounds  which is challenging for the students  they actually have to engage in  dissemination and activities as a group  so they are not their typical PhD  students where they have a one-to-one  relationship with their supervisor and  we have a state-of-the-art scientific  facility which is a mobile heritage  laboratory which is not only enables  student to do advanced analytical  techniques in the field but it is also a  vehicle for public engagement so its  main purpose is to engage the public in  heritage science so finally the close  collaboration with the partners is  providing students with valuable  transferable skills from project  management to negotiation skills through  having to negotiate among the  requirements of different  stakeholders and also time management  and in fact for partners a modest  investment can deliver considerable  research benefits and I finish with a  quotation from one of our industry  partners who has said of one of our  students the solid ground work that hend  who is one of our Egyptian students has  developed and proven at UCL is now being  converted to high efficiency directly  coded algorithms at the Gildan photonics  which means that her work has actually  enabled them to improve their imaging  capability and the service that they  provide to clients which is great  because that's what we want to do we  want to show that the students are  relevant thank you
 

